#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:1
msgid "Inlining"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:3
msgid ""
"There are several options that control which calls the analyzer will "
"consider for inlining. The major one is `-analyzer-config ipa`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:6
msgid ""
"`analyzer-config ipa=none` - All inlining is disabled. This is the only mode "
"available in LLVM 3.1 and earlier and in Xcode 4.3 and earlier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:8
msgid ""
"`analyzer-config ipa=basic-inlining` - Turns on inlining for C functions, C++"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:9
msgid ""
"static member functions, and blocks -- essentially, the calls that behave "
"like simple C function calls. This is essentially the mode used in Xcode 4.4."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:12
msgid ""
"`analyzer-config ipa=inlining` - Turns on inlining when we can confidently "
"find"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:13
msgid ""
"the function/method body corresponding to the call. (C functions, static "
"functions, devirtualized C++ methods, Objective-C class methods, Objective-C "
"instance methods when ExprEngine is confident about the dynamic type of the "
"instance)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:17
msgid ""
"`analyzer-config ipa=dynamic` - Inline instance methods for which the type is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:18
msgid ""
"determined at runtime and we are not 100% sure that our type info is "
"correct. For virtual calls, inline the most plausible definition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:20
msgid ""
"`analyzer-config ipa=dynamic-bifurcate` - Same as -analyzer-config "
"ipa=dynamic,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:21
msgid ""
"but the path is split. We inline on one branch and do not inline on the "
"other. This mode does not drop the coverage in cases when the parent class "
"has code that is only exercised when some of its methods are overridden."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:25
msgid ""
"Currently, `-analyzer-config ipa=dynamic-bifurcate` is the default mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:27
msgid ""
"While `-analyzer-config ipa` determines in general how aggressively the "
"analyzer will try to inline functions, several additional options control "
"which types of functions can inlined, in an all-or-nothing way. These "
"options use the analyzer's configuration table, so they are all specified as "
"follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:32
msgid "`-analyzer-config OPTION=VALUE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:34
msgid "c++-inlining"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:36
msgid "This option controls which C++ member functions may be inlined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:38
msgid ""
"`-analyzer-config c++-inlining=[none | methods | constructors | destructors]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:40
msgid ""
"Each of these modes implies that all the previous member function kinds will "
"be inlined as well; it doesn't make sense to inline destructors without "
"inlining constructors, for example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:44
msgid ""
"The default c++-inlining mode is 'destructors', meaning that all member "
"functions with visible definitions will be considered for inlining. In some "
"cases the analyzer may still choose not to inline the function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:48
msgid ""
"Note that under 'constructors', constructors for types with non-trivial "
"destructors will not be inlined. Additionally, no C++ member functions will "
"be inlined under -analyzer-config ipa=none or -analyzer-config ipa=basic-"
"inlining, regardless of the setting of the c++-inlining mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:53
msgid "c++-template-inlining"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:55
msgid "This option controls whether C++ templated functions may be inlined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:57
msgid "`-analyzer-config c++-template-inlining=[true | false]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:59
msgid "Currently, template functions are considered for inlining by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:61
msgid ""
"The motivation behind this option is that very generic code can be a source "
"of false positives, either by considering paths that the caller considers "
"impossible (by some unstated precondition), or by inlining some but not all "
"of a deep implementation of a function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:66
msgid "c++-stdlib-inlining"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:68
msgid ""
"This option controls whether functions from the C++ standard library, "
"including methods of the container classes in the Standard Template Library, "
"should be considered for inlining."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:72
msgid "`-analyzer-config c++-stdlib-inlining=[true | false]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:74
msgid ""
"Currently, C++ standard library functions are considered for inlining by "
"default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:77
msgid ""
"The standard library functions and the STL in particular are used "
"ubiquitously enough that our tolerance for false positives is even lower "
"here. A false positive due to poor modeling of the STL leads to a poor user "
"experience, since most users would not be comfortable adding assertions to "
"system headers in order to silence analyzer warnings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:83
msgid "c++-container-inlining"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:85
msgid ""
"This option controls whether constructors and destructors of \"container\" "
"types should be considered for inlining."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:88
msgid "`-analyzer-config c++-container-inlining=[true | false]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:90
msgid ""
"Currently, these constructors and destructors are NOT considered for "
"inlining by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:93
msgid ""
"The current implementation of this setting checks whether a type has a "
"member named 'iterator' or a member named 'begin'; these names are idiomatic "
"in C++, with the latter specified in the C++11 standard. The analyzer "
"currently does a fairly poor job of modeling certain data structure "
"invariants of container-like objects. For example, these three expressions "
"should be equivalent:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:105
msgid ""
"Many of these issues are avoided if containers always have unknown, symbolic "
"state, which is what happens when their constructors are treated as opaque. "
"In the future, we may decide specific containers are \"safe\" to model "
"through inlining, or choose to model them directly using checkers instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:110
msgid "Basics of Implementation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:112
msgid ""
"The low-level mechanism of inlining a function is handled in ExprEngine::"
"inlineCall and ExprEngine::processCallExit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:115
msgid ""
"If the conditions are right for inlining, a CallEnter node is created and "
"added to the analysis work list. The CallEnter node marks the change to a "
"new StackFrame representing the called function, and its state includes the "
"contents of the new stack frame. When the CallEnter node is actually "
"processed, its single successor will be an edge to the first CFG block in "
"the function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:121
msgid ""
"Exiting an inlined function is a bit more work, fortunately broken up into "
"reasonable steps:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:124
msgid ""
"The CoreEngine realizes we're at the end of an inlined call and generates a "
"CallExitBegin node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:126
msgid ""
"ExprEngine takes over (in processCallExit) and finds the return value of the "
"function, if it has one. This is bound to the expression that triggered the "
"call. (In the case of calls without origin expressions, such as destructors, "
"this step is skipped.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:130
msgid ""
"Dead symbols and bindings are cleaned out from the state, including any "
"local bindings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:132
msgid ""
"A CallExitEnd node is generated, which marks the transition back to the "
"caller's StackFrame."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:134
msgid ""
"Custom post-call checks are processed and the final nodes are pushed back "
"onto the work list, so that evaluation of the caller can continue."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:137
msgid "Retry Without Inlining"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:139
msgid ""
"In some cases, we would like to retry analysis without inlining a particular "
"call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:142
msgid ""
"Currently, we use this technique to recover coverage in case we stop "
"analyzing a path due to exceeding the maximum block count inside an inlined "
"function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:146
msgid ""
"When this situation is detected, we walk up the path to find the first node "
"before inlining was started and enqueue it on the WorkList with a special "
"ReplayWithoutInlining bit added to it (ExprEngine::replayWithoutInlining). "
"The path is then re-analyzed from that point without inlining that "
"particular call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:151
msgid "Deciding When to Inline"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:153
msgid ""
"In general, the analyzer attempts to inline as much as possible, since it "
"provides a better summary of what actually happens in the program. There are "
"some cases, however, where the analyzer chooses not to inline:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:157
msgid ""
"If there is no definition available for the called function or method. In "
"this case, there is no opportunity to inline."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:160
msgid ""
"If the CFG cannot be constructed for a called function, or the liveness "
"cannot be computed. These are prerequisites for analyzing a function body, "
"with or without inlining."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:164
msgid ""
"If the StackFrame chain for a given ExplodedNode reaches a maximum cutoff "
"depth. This prevents unbounded analysis due to infinite recursion, but also "
"serves as a useful cutoff for performance reasons."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:168
msgid ""
"If the function is variadic. This is not a hard limitation, but an "
"engineering limitation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:171
msgid ""
"Tracked by: `<rdar://problem/12147064>` Support inlining of variadic "
"functions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:173
msgid ""
"In C++, constructors are not inlined unless the destructor call will be "
"processed by the ExprEngine. Thus, if the CFG was built without nodes for "
"implicit destructors, or if the destructors for the given object are not "
"represented in the CFG, the constructor will not be inlined. (As an "
"exception, constructors for objects with trivial constructors can still be "
"inlined.) See \"C++ Caveats\" below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:180
msgid ""
"In C++, ExprEngine does not inline custom implementations of operator 'new' "
"or operator 'delete', nor does it inline the constructors and destructors "
"associated with these. See \"C++ Caveats\" below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:184
msgid ""
"Calls resulting in \"dynamic dispatch\" are specially handled. See more "
"below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:186
msgid ""
"The FunctionSummaries map stores additional information about declarations, "
"some of which is collected at runtime based on previous analyses. We do not "
"inline functions which were not profitable to inline in a different context "
"(for example, if the maximum block count was exceeded; see \"Retry Without "
"Inlining\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:192
msgid "Dynamic Calls and Devirtualization"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:194
msgid ""
"\"Dynamic\" calls are those that are resolved at runtime, such as C++ "
"virtual method calls and Objective-C message sends. Due to the path-"
"sensitive nature of the analysis, the analyzer may be able to reason about "
"the dynamic type of the object whose method is being called and thus "
"\"devirtualize\" the call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:199
msgid ""
"This path-sensitive devirtualization occurs when the analyzer can determine "
"what method would actually be called at runtime. This is possible when the "
"type information is constrained enough for a simulated C++/Objective-C "
"object that the analyzer can make such a decision."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:204
msgid "DynamicTypeInfo"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:206
msgid ""
"As the analyzer analyzes a path, it may accrue information to refine the "
"knowledge about the type of an object. This can then be used to make better "
"decisions about the target method of a call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:210
msgid ""
"Such type information is tracked as DynamicTypeInfo. This is path-sensitive "
"data that is stored in ProgramState, which defines a mapping from MemRegions "
"to an (optional) DynamicTypeInfo."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:214
msgid ""
"If no DynamicTypeInfo has been explicitly set for a MemRegion, it will be "
"lazily inferred from the region's type or associated symbol. Information "
"from symbolic regions is weaker than from true typed regions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:218
msgid ""
"EXAMPLE: A C++ object declared \"A obj\" is known to have the class 'A', but "
"a"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:220
msgid "reference \"A &ref\" may dynamically be a subclass of 'A'."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:222
msgid ""
"The DynamicTypePropagation checker gathers and propagates DynamicTypeInfo, "
"updating it as information is observed along a path that can refine that "
"type information for a region."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:226
msgid ""
"WARNING: Not all of the existing analyzer code has been retrofitted to use"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:228
msgid ""
"DynamicTypeInfo, nor is it universally appropriate. In particular, "
"DynamicTypeInfo always applies to a region with all casts stripped off, but "
"sometimes the information provided by casts can be useful."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:232
msgid "RuntimeDefinition"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:234
msgid ""
"The basis of devirtualization is CallEvent's getRuntimeDefinition() method, "
"which returns a RuntimeDefinition object. When asked to provide a "
"definition, the CallEvents for dynamic calls will use the DynamicTypeInfo in "
"their ProgramState to attempt to devirtualize the call. In the case of no "
"dynamic dispatch, or perfectly constrained devirtualization, the resulting "
"RuntimeDefinition contains a Decl corresponding to the definition of the "
"called function, and RuntimeDefinition::mayHaveOtherDefinitions will return "
"FALSE."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:242
msgid ""
"In the case of dynamic dispatch where our information is not perfect, "
"CallEvent can make a guess, but RuntimeDefinition::mayHaveOtherDefinitions "
"will return TRUE. The RuntimeDefinition object will then also include a "
"MemRegion corresponding to the object being called (i.e., the \"receiver\" "
"in Objective-C parlance), which ExprEngine uses to decide whether or not the "
"call should be inlined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:249
msgid "Inlining Dynamic Calls"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:251
msgid ""
"The -analyzer-config ipa option has five different modes: none, basic-"
"inlining, inlining, dynamic, and dynamic-bifurcate. Under -analyzer-config "
"ipa=dynamic, all dynamic calls are inlined, whether we are certain or not "
"that this will actually be the definition used at runtime. Under -analyzer-"
"config ipa=inlining, only \"near-perfect\" devirtualized calls are inlined*, "
"and other dynamic calls are evaluated conservatively (as if no definition "
"were available)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:258
msgid ""
"Currently, no Objective-C messages are not inlined under -analyzer-config "
"ipa=inlining, even if we are reasonably confident of the type of the "
"receiver. We plan to enable this once we have tested our heuristics more "
"thoroughly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:263
msgid ""
"The last option, -analyzer-config ipa=dynamic-bifurcate, behaves similarly "
"to \"dynamic\", but performs a conservative invalidation in the general "
"virtual case in *addition* to inlining. The details of this are discussed "
"below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:267
msgid ""
"As stated above, -analyzer-config ipa=basic-inlining does not inline any C++ "
"member functions or Objective-C method calls, even if they are non-virtual "
"or can be safely devirtualized."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:271
msgid "Bifurcation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:273
msgid ""
"ExprEngine::BifurcateCall implements the `-analyzer-config ipa=dynamic-"
"bifurcate` mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:276
msgid ""
"When a call is made on an object with imprecise dynamic type information "
"(RuntimeDefinition::mayHaveOtherDefinitions() evaluates to TRUE), ExprEngine "
"bifurcates the path and marks the object's region (retrieved from the "
"RuntimeDefinition object) with a path-sensitive \"mode\" in the ProgramState."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:281
msgid "Currently, there are 2 modes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:283
msgid ""
"`DynamicDispatchModeInlined` - Models the case where the dynamic type "
"information"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:284
msgid ""
"of the receiver (MemoryRegion) is assumed to be perfectly constrained so "
"that a given definition of a method is expected to be the code actually "
"called. When this mode is set, ExprEngine uses the Decl from "
"RuntimeDefinition to inline any dynamically dispatched call sent to this "
"receiver because the function definition is considered to be fully resolved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:289
msgid ""
"`DynamicDispatchModeConservative` - Models the case where the dynamic type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:290
msgid ""
"information is assumed to be incorrect, for example, implies that the method "
"definition is overridden in a subclass. In such cases, ExprEngine does not "
"inline the methods sent to the receiver (MemoryRegion), even if a candidate "
"definition is available. This mode is conservative about simulating the "
"effects of a call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:296
msgid ""
"Going forward along the symbolic execution path, ExprEngine consults the "
"mode of the receiver's MemRegion to make decisions on whether the calls "
"should be inlined or not, which ensures that there is at most one split per "
"region."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:300
msgid ""
"At a high level, \"bifurcation mode\" allows for increased semantic coverage "
"in cases where the parent method contains code which is only executed when "
"the class is subclassed. The disadvantages of this mode are a "
"(considerable?) performance hit and the possibility of false positives on "
"the path where the conservative mode is used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:306
msgid "Objective-C Message Heuristics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:308
msgid ""
"ExprEngine relies on a set of heuristics to partition the set of Objective-C "
"method calls into those that require bifurcation and those that do not. "
"Below are the cases when the DynamicTypeInfo of the object is considered "
"precise (cannot be a subclass):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:313
msgid ""
"If the object was created with +alloc or +new and initialized with an -init "
"method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:315
msgid ""
"If the calls are property accesses using dot syntax. This is based on the "
"assumption that children rarely override properties, or do so in an "
"essentially compatible way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:318
msgid ""
"If the class interface is declared inside the main source file. In this case "
"it is unlikely that it will be subclassed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:320
msgid ""
"If the method is not declared outside of main source file, either by the "
"receiver's class or by any superclasses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:323
msgid "C++ Caveats"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:325
msgid ""
"C++11 [class.cdtor]p4 describes how the vtable of an object is modified as "
"it is being constructed or destructed; that is, the type of the object "
"depends on which base constructors have been completed. This is tracked "
"using DynamicTypeInfo in the DynamicTypePropagation checker."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:330
msgid "There are several limitations in the current implementation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:332
msgid ""
"Temporaries are poorly modeled right now because we're not confident in the "
"placement of their destructors in the CFG. We currently won't inline their "
"constructors unless the destructor is trivial, and don't process their "
"destructors at all, not even to invalidate the region."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:336
msgid ""
"'new' is poorly modeled due to some nasty CFG/design issues. This is tracked "
"in PR12014. 'delete' is not modeled at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:338
msgid ""
"Arrays of objects are modeled very poorly right now. ExprEngine currently "
"only simulates the first constructor and first destructor. Because of this, "
"ExprEngine does not inline any constructors or destructors for arrays."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:342
msgid "CallEvent"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:344
msgid ""
"A CallEvent represents a specific call to a function, method, or other body "
"of code. It is path-sensitive, containing both the current state "
"(ProgramStateRef) and stack space (StackFrame), and provides uniform access "
"to the argument values and return type of a call, no matter how the call is "
"written in the source or what sort of code body is being invoked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:350
msgid "NOTE: For those familiar with Cocoa, CallEvent is roughly equivalent to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:352
msgid "NSInvocation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:354
msgid ""
"CallEvent should be used whenever there is logic dealing with function calls "
"that does not care how the call occurred."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:357
msgid ""
"Examples include checking that arguments satisfy preconditions (such as "
"`__attribute__((nonnull))`), and attempting to inline a call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../analyzer/developer-docs/IPA.md:360
msgid ""
"CallEvents are reference-counted objects managed by a CallEventManager. "
"While there is no inherent issue with persisting them (say, in a "
"ProgramState's GDM), they are intended for short-lived use, and can be "
"recreated from CFGElements or non-top-level StackFrames fairly easily."
msgstr ""
